1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,740
Last time, we saw day, night, day, night.

2
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:06,620
That is a cycle, and it repeats because the Earth turns.

3
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:09,340
But do living things have cycles too?

4
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:15,300
A little ladybird is hiding in this video.

5
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:16,790
Can you spot it?

6
00:00:16,912 --> 00:00:19,012
Maybe it is on a leaf, or on a flower.

7
00:00:19,242 --> 00:00:20,352
Keep looking!

8
00:00:20,478 --> 00:00:22,318
Lock in first with your fingers.

9
00:00:22,588 --> 00:00:24,418
One finger for every point.

10
00:00:25,005 --> 00:00:26,155
Today you'll learn:

11
00:00:26,352 --> 00:00:27,942
Cycles in living things.

12
00:00:28,139 --> 00:00:29,599
What a life cycle is.

13
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:31,390
Why life cycles matter.

14
00:00:33,138 --> 00:00:34,018
Round one.

15
00:00:34,328 --> 00:00:38,098
Long ago, on Tree Planting Day, a boy planted a young tree.

16
00:00:38,378 --> 00:00:40,208
His teddy bear sat beside it.

17
00:00:40,478 --> 00:00:42,228
Today, which ones are bigger?

18
00:00:42,528 --> 00:00:44,218
One, the boy and the tree.

19
00:00:44,488 --> 00:00:45,708
Two, all three.

20
00:00:45,978 --> 00:00:47,448
Three, only the tree.

21
00:00:47,738 --> 00:00:49,208
Show me with your fingers.

22
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:56,660
Locked in!

23
00:00:56,788 --> 00:00:57,568
Two!

24
00:00:57,648 --> 00:00:59,628
Everything gets bigger with time!

25
00:00:59,674 --> 00:01:03,204
One! A teddy bear is non-living, so it cannot grow!

26
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:06,260
One. I think only living things grow.

27
00:01:07,213 --> 00:01:08,363
Look closer.

28
00:01:08,953 --> 00:01:11,403
Here is the same place, then and now.

29
00:01:11,527 --> 00:01:12,147
Whoa!

30
00:01:12,297 --> 00:01:14,817
The boy is a man now, and he is much taller!

31
00:01:15,127 --> 00:01:17,987
And the young tree is a tall tree now, with fruits.

32
00:01:18,299 --> 00:01:20,459
But the teddy bear is the same size.

33
00:01:20,529 --> 00:01:21,899
Why did it not grow?

34
00:01:22,209 --> 00:01:23,939
Because it is non-living.

35
00:01:24,159 --> 00:01:28,109
Living things grow over time, and non-living things do not.

36
00:01:28,621 --> 00:01:32,041
Growing means getting bigger, like my height marks on the door!

37
00:01:34,665 --> 00:01:37,775
The answer is one: the boy and the tree grew bigger!

38
00:01:38,065 --> 00:01:39,025
One finger?

39
00:01:39,345 --> 00:01:40,545
One finger up!

40
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:42,720
Look closer.

41
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:44,670
Here is one chick.

42
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:47,010
Week after week, it grows.

43
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:51,920
It is so big now.

44
00:01:52,070 --> 00:01:53,700
Is it an adult chicken?

45
00:01:53,914 --> 00:01:54,764
Yes!

46
00:01:55,014 --> 00:01:57,654
It is fully grown, so we call it an adult.

47
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:02,690
And look! It has chicks of its own.

48
00:02:02,850 --> 00:02:04,370
Its young are its babies!

49
00:02:04,481 --> 00:02:07,261
Living things reproduce and have young.

50
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:09,423
What does reproduce mean?

51
00:02:09,443 --> 00:02:11,553
It means making more of your own kind.

52
00:02:11,703 --> 00:02:13,243
Now there are more chickens!

53
00:02:15,867 --> 00:02:19,987
Cycles in living things happen when living things grow and have young.

54
00:02:20,257 --> 00:02:23,757
Their life cycles follow a repeated pattern of change.

55
00:02:24,491 --> 00:02:25,421
Be a chick!

56
00:02:25,731 --> 00:02:26,721
Shrink down.

57
00:02:26,991 --> 00:02:27,931
Quietly.

58
00:02:30,213 --> 00:02:32,393
You are a chick, inside an egg.

59
00:02:32,905 --> 00:02:33,675
Crack!

60
00:02:34,495 --> 00:02:36,525
Light comes in, and you hatch.

61
00:02:37,043 --> 00:02:39,693
You eat, and you grow, week after week.

62
00:02:40,199 --> 00:02:42,059
Now you are an adult chicken.

63
00:02:42,839 --> 00:02:44,089
You lay an egg.

64
00:02:44,596 --> 00:02:45,966
Tap, tap, tap.

65
00:02:46,726 --> 00:02:49,566
A new chick hatches, and round we go again!

66
00:02:51,349 --> 00:02:54,176
First an egg, then a chick, then an adult chicken, and then

67
00:02:54,196 --> 00:02:55,070
an egg again!

68
00:02:55,153 --> 00:02:58,403
It goes round and round: that is a life cycle!

69
00:02:59,783 --> 00:03:00,943
Say it with me!

70
00:03:01,651 --> 00:03:05,031
A life cycle is a repeated pattern of change as a living

71
00:03:05,051 --> 00:03:07,861
thing grows over time and reproduces.

72
00:03:11,490 --> 00:03:12,400
Showdown!

73
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:13,830
Double points.

74
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:15,910
Pip's notes have one mistake.

75
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:17,970
Line one, two, or three?

76
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:19,730
Show me with your fingers.

77
00:03:19,795 --> 00:03:21,135
Here are my notes!

78
00:03:21,285 --> 00:03:23,955
Two: a life cycle ends at the adult.

79
00:03:30,566 --> 00:03:31,546
Locked in!

80
00:03:31,674 --> 00:03:32,284
Two!

81
00:03:32,434 --> 00:03:35,094
The adult lays an egg, so round it goes again!

82
00:03:35,141 --> 00:03:35,961
Two!

83
00:03:36,021 --> 00:03:39,421
A life cycle repeats, so it does not end at the adult!

84
00:03:41,085 --> 00:03:42,125
Two fingers?

85
00:03:42,365 --> 00:03:44,295
Put up two more on your score hand!

86
00:03:45,318 --> 00:03:46,386
Look closer.

87
00:03:46,406 --> 00:03:49,886
It is like a chicken family photo: a grandmother hen, a mother

88
00:03:49,906 --> 00:03:51,146
hen and a chick!

89
00:03:51,166 --> 00:03:54,456
So chickens come again and again, all of the same kind!

90
00:03:57,904 --> 00:03:59,194
Say it with me!

91
00:03:59,909 --> 00:04:04,029
Life cycles ensure that living things of the same kind can continue

92
00:04:04,049 --> 00:04:05,269
to live on Earth.

93
00:04:08,034 --> 00:04:09,139
Say it with us!

94
00:04:09,799 --> 00:04:10,812
Day, night!

95
00:04:11,379 --> 00:04:12,602
Round and round!

96
00:04:16,199 --> 00:04:17,187
Egg, chick!

97
00:04:17,779 --> 00:04:19,002
Round and round!

98
00:04:22,599 --> 00:04:23,850
Change, repeat!

99
00:04:24,179 --> 00:04:25,388
That's a cycle!

100
00:04:29,754 --> 00:04:31,224
Show me your score hand!

101
00:04:31,504 --> 00:04:32,554
More than one?

102
00:04:32,854 --> 00:04:34,214
You beat the Buddies.

103
00:04:34,514 --> 00:04:35,574
Three fingers?

104
00:04:35,844 --> 00:04:36,854
High five!

105
00:04:37,359 --> 00:04:38,959
Did you spot the ladybird?

106
00:04:39,259 --> 00:04:41,229
It was on a leaf of the young tree!

107
00:04:41,368 --> 00:04:42,958
Here is a fun fact!

108
00:04:43,158 --> 00:04:45,998
A baby ladybird looks nothing like its parent.

109
00:04:46,158 --> 00:04:49,798
It hatches as a spiky larva, like a tiny crocodile.

110
00:04:50,028 --> 00:04:52,828
Then it becomes a pupa, and then a ladybird!

111
00:04:52,952 --> 00:04:54,232
Tell someone at home!

112
00:04:55,021 --> 00:04:56,721
Next time: a mango!

113
00:04:57,001 --> 00:04:59,251
Inside it, there is one big seed.

114
00:04:59,531 --> 00:05:00,801
We plant the seed.

115
00:05:01,111 --> 00:05:02,691
What will it grow into first?

116
00:05:02,961 --> 00:05:04,241
One, a mango.

117
00:05:04,521 --> 00:05:05,701
Two, a flower.

118
00:05:06,001 --> 00:05:07,501
Three, a young plant.

119
00:05:07,781 --> 00:05:09,101
Lock in your answer!

120
00:05:09,376 --> 00:05:11,576
Let's read today's key words together!

121
00:05:11,866 --> 00:05:12,626
Ready?

122
00:05:12,913 --> 00:05:13,643
Grow.

123
00:05:14,956 --> 00:05:15,809
Reproduce.

124
00:05:17,429 --> 00:05:18,579
Life cycle.

125
00:05:20,137 --> 00:05:21,077
Well done!

126
00:05:22,059 --> 00:05:23,160
Say it with us!

127
00:05:23,961 --> 00:05:24,711
Ready?

128
00:05:24,834 --> 00:05:25,494
One!

129
00:05:25,834 --> 00:05:26,534
two!

130
00:05:26,839 --> 00:05:28,014
Now you know!
